    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.transitive_closure - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.transitive_closure.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.transitive_closure()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the transitive closure of a directed graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.transitive_closure()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the transitive closure of a directed graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.transitive_closure()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute the transitive closure of a directed graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the transitive closure of a directed graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L74428-L74535){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.transitive_closure()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Transitive closure](../assets/graphs/transitive-closure.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A directed chain A to B to C to D. The transitive closure adds an accent arrow for every pair joined by a longer path: A to C, B to D, and A to D.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe transitive closure is a graph where there is an edge `(u, v)` if and only if there is a\npath from `u` to `v` in the original graph. This is useful for reachability analysis,\ndetermining which nodes can reach which other nodes.\n\nFor example, if there's a path `A -> B -> C` in the original graph, the transitive closure\nwill have edges `A -> B`, `B -> C`, and `A -> C`.\n\nThis method only works on directed graphs. Undirected graphs should first be converted to\ndirected or analyzed using connectivity methods.\n\nTime complexity: `O(V × (V + E))` where `V` is nodes and `E` is edges\nSpace complexity: `O(V + E')` where `E'` is edges in the closure\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   New directed graph representing the transitive closure.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If called on an undirected graph.\n:::\n\n## Common Use Cases {.doc-common-use-cases}\n\n- determining all reachable nodes from a given node\n- computing transitive dependencies in build systems\n- checking if one node can influence another in a network\n- preprocessing for constant-time reachability queries\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nClosure and reduction are duals on DAGs: applying `transitive_closure()` to\n`transitive_reduction(G)` recovers the same edge set as applying it to $G$ directly,\nsince both share exactly the same reachability relation. The construction here does\na BFS from each node in $O(V(V + E))$ time and stores the result as a fresh graph\nwhose edge set $E'$ can grow to $\\Theta(V^2)$ in the dense-reachability limit. Once\nbuilt, reachability queries collapse to a single `has_edge()` lookup, which is why\ntransitive closure is a common preprocessing step for constant-time ancestor or\ndependency queries.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~transitive_reduction`) \n\n:   The fewest edges with the same reachability.\n\n[](`~descendants`) \n\n:   Every node reachable from a node.\n\n[](`~ancestors`) \n\n:   Every node that can reach a node.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe closure adds an arrow wherever one node reaches another through others:\n\n::: {#6659f45f .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\")], directed=True\n)\nclosure = graph.transitive_closure()\n\ngraph.count_edges(), closure.count_edges(), closure.has_edge(\"A\", \"D\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(3, 6, True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Everything a course depends on\n\n`courses()` holds the courses of a made-up computer science program, with an arrow from\neach course to every course that requires it. Its closure has an arrow from each course to\nevery course that needs it, directly or not, so arrows into a course count everything that\nmust come before it. CS460 needs 9 courses, and CS101 is needed by 11:\n\n::: {#6f32ffd8 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncourses = ec.courses()\nclosure = courses.transitive_closure()\nbefore = {n: closure.get_in_degree(n) for n in closure.get_node_ids()}\n\n(\n    closure.count_edges(),\n    closure.get_in_degree(\"CS460\"),\n    closure.get_out_degree(\"CS101\"),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(51, 9, 11)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nSized by how many courses come before them, the courses grow toward the end of the\nprogram:\n\n::: {#2dfcab77 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nstages = dict(enumerate(courses.topological_generations()))\ncourses = courses.set_node_attrs(\"before\", before)\n\ncourses.layout_multipartite(subset_key=stages).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", size_by=\"before\", color_by=\"dept\",\n    label_by=\"label\", height=420,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"420\" viewBox=\"47.5 16.7 682.2 409.3\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"47.5\" y=\"16.7\" width=